// endpoint_report_editor_focus.proto — ReportEditorFocus: Emacs gained or
// lost desktop focus. HOST section.
//
// Emacs is the only sender. It calls this on every focus change its
// `after-focus-change-function` observes; the focus it holds when it CONNECTS
// rides WatchDaemonEmacs instead, so the daemon never stands on an Emacs
// stream without knowing its focus. The daemon replaces its held focus with
// the reported one and decides every later desktop banner on it.
//
// A FOCUS GAIN CLAIMS THE NEWEST BANNER, IN THE SAME CALL. A report that moves
// the stream's held focus from unfocused to focused is Emacs coming forward,
// whatever brought it (the dock, an application switcher, a click on one of
// its frames). The daemon answers it with the workspace of the newest desktop
// banner it holds, which Emacs selects exactly as any other switch (it
// REQUESTS the switch with SelectWorkspace and its frame moves on the
// request), and lets go of every banner it held. The claim is made under the
// very focus change that justifies it, so no unfocused report can land
// between the gain and the claim, and the switch costs one round trip.
//
// A BANNER IS HELD from the moment it is made until a claim, a workspace
// selection (a banner's own click is one), or a newer banner of its workspace
// lets it go; a read-back banner (Linux notify-send) is let go too once its
// program stops showing it. The platform is NOT asked at the claim which
// banners it still lists: that query is a program run (terminal-notifier
// -list, 40-160ms) on the path from focus to switch, and the switch is due in
// tens of milliseconds. So a macOS banner the user cleared from Notification
// Center still steers the next return.
//
// A CLAIM CONSUMES: each banner steers at most one return to Emacs, and none
// made before the user last chose a workspace ever steers one.
//
// IDEMPOTENT for the held focus: reporting the focus the daemon already holds
// is a success, changes nothing, and claims nothing.

